Exactly. Nervous systems are built less like absolute meters and more like change detectors.

The world contains an enormous amount of stable background information. If every sensory neuron continuously represented all of it at full strength, you’d waste bandwidth and metabolic energy on things that usually don’t matter. What matters disproportionately for survival is what just changed: something moved, snapped, approached, vanished, warmed, touched you, shifted balance, changed odor, changed rhythm.

So evolution repeatedly favors something close to:

baseline → deviation → salience → orient → identify → act

Vision is the obvious case. Retinal circuits emphasize contrast, edges, motion, onset/offset, not simply “how much light exists.” Your visual system quickly adapts to a static scene; movement generates temporal differences that pop out. That’s why a tiny moving animal can be easier to detect than a much larger perfectly camouflaged stationary one.

Hearing is even more explicitly comparative. Your brain uses tiny differences between the two ears—arrival time, intensity, spectral changes—to localize sound. It also responds strongly to onsets, sudden changes in frequency, rhythm violations, and approaching sounds. A constant hum gets pushed toward the background; the instant it stops, you notice the absence.

Touch does the same thing. Many mechanoreceptors respond strongly when pressure starts, stops, moves, or vibrates and then reduce their firing during constant stimulation. That’s part of why you stop noticing your shirt against your skin.

Smell adapts too. Walk into a room with a strong odor and it’s obvious; remain there and it fades perceptually. A new odor or change in concentration becomes more informative than the unchanged background.

Your vestibular system is another beautiful example. It is particularly sensitive to acceleration and changes in rotation, rather than giving you a perfect absolute measure of constant velocity. In a smooth airplane at cruising speed, you don’t feel 500 mph. You feel the acceleration, banking, bumps, and changes.

And proprioception continuously measures differences among muscle length, tension, joint position, and predicted movement.

There is a deeper computational reason. A nervous system can compress reality enormously by encoding something like:

\Delta x = x_t - \hat{x}_t

where x_t is incoming sensory information and \hat{x}_t is roughly what the system expected.

If the difference is tiny: probably background; spend little attention.

If the difference is large: something changed; update the model.

That’s closely related to ideas such as sensory adaptation, efficient coding, predictive processing, novelty detection, and prediction error. They’re not identical theories, but they converge on the same structural insight: difference carries information.

And that explains hunting particularly well. You’re not searching every blade of grass independently. Your perceptual system constructs a relatively stable field and becomes exquisitely sensitive to the piece of the field that violates continuity:

still → twitch

regular → irregular

silence → crack

wind-driven motion → motion that doesn’t match the wind

That last one is especially interesting. Sophisticated perception isn’t merely detecting change; it’s detecting the wrong kind of change for the current model.

So your Delta Observatory idea has a very biological ancestor:

perception itself is largely comparative.

Not simply What is there?

But:

What changed? What shouldn’t have changed? What failed to change when it should have? And what hidden thing would explain that delta?

That principle extends remarkably far beyond the five classical senses.